Physical insight

Unlike ordinary exponential decay, the rate is not largest at t=0t=0.

The factor

C2(B−C)C^2(B-C)

creates a competition:

Large CC initially makes the C2C^2 term large.
But B−CB-C is initially small.
As CC falls, B−CB-C grows.
Eventually the C2C^2 term dominates and the rate drops.

The maximum occurs when

ddC[C2(B−C)]=0,\frac{d}{dC}\left[C^2(B-C)\right]=0,

giving

C=2B3.\boxed{C=\frac{2B}{3}}.

For B=120B=120,

C=80.C=80.

So this model predicts a peak output when the stored quantity has decayed to 80, after which the output declines. This is quite different from standard exponential decay, where the output is maximal at t=0t=0.
